{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0161BymtaeuBCkjSiukR4UfM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tYou are the person who asked for this diagnosis. An analyst has sent you the brief below. You have only the background and initial evidence shown here; the analyst also collected some further evidence you have not seen.\n2\tRate the brief:\n3\t- persuasiveness (1-7): how convinced are you by it?\n4\t- perceivedCorrectness (0-100): how likely do you think its diagnosis is correct?\n5\t- actionability (1-7): how clearly does it tell you what to do next?\n6\tGive a one-sentence reason. Judge the brief as a decision-maker would; you do not know the true answer.\n7\t\n8\tBackground: Brindlemoor Freight runs an internal pricing service called ratequote. It returns shipping quotes to the customer booking web app, to the partner-sync integration jobs and to internal reporting. It handles about 2.1 million requests a week behind an internal load balancer and is owned by the Pricing Platform team. The team's error metric is the share of requests that return an HTTP 5xx status. Over 24 pods, the service reads from a quote cache and falls back to a read replica of the pricing database. It also calls an upstream tariff-rates service owned by another team.\n9\tYour request: On Tuesday 3 November 2026 the Director of Platform Engineering asked two questions: did release v4.18 cause last week's rise in ratequote's 5xx rate, and should it be rolled back today? You are asked what, if anything, explains the rise and what the team should do.\n10\tInitial evidence:\n11\t- Email from Marguerite Osei-Lund, Director of Platform Engineering, to the Pricing Platform team, Tue 3 Nov 2026 (leadership-account): Subject: ratequote errors. 'ratequote's 5xx rate went from 0.31% to 0.41% last week. That is a 34% jump, and it happened right after v4.18 shipped on Tuesday. Dario's rollout numbers show the new pods erroring at almost twice the rate of the old ones, so I think we know what happened. I want v4.18 rolled back today. Someone in the channel also suggested muting all of ratequote's 5xx alerting, burn-rate page included, for a month until things settle. I'm open to that if the noise is distracting people.'\n12\t- Export of dashboard 'ratequote / errors', default four-week view, taken Mon 2 Nov (telemetry): Source: internal load balancer access logs. Weekly 5xx rate, weeks starting Monday: 5 Oct 0.29%; 12 Oct 0.32%; 19 Oct 0.31% (6,410 of 2,086,000 requests); 26 Oct 0.41% (8,690 of 2,112,000). Request volume in the week of 26 Oct was 1.2% above the prior week. Seven-day error-budget burn: 0.82x. Note added by SRE Fen Achterberg: 'I recounted 5xx lines directly from the raw load balancer log files for both weeks and got 6,402 and 8,677. The small gaps are late-shipped log batches. Nobody has edited the dashboard query since March.'\n13\t- Release record RQ-4.18, filed by release engineer Dario Kestrel (change-record): Deployed Tue 27 Oct as a rolling update. Started 14:10, completed 15:02 with all 24 pods on v4.18. Contents: HTTP client library fetchwell bumped from 5.2 to 5.4; new optional 'surcharge_breakdown' response field behind a flag (flag off); removal of the unused /v1/legacy-quote endpoint. Rollout observation (my note): 'During the rollout I compared 5xx on v4.18 pods with v4.17 pods. v4.18: 0.52% (31 of 5,960 requests). v4.17: 0.30% (22 of 7,330). Same minutes, same traffic. That looks like the new version to me, and my guess is the fetchwell bump.'\n14\t- Change calendar, ratequote and dependencies, 26 Oct–1 Nov (change-record): Tue 27 Oct 14:10: ratequote v4.18 (RQ-4.18). Wed 28 Oct 09:30: ratequote's read replica moved from host class m-std to host class m-dense (DB-2291). The previous hosts are kept until 20 Nov as a fallback. No load balancer, gateway or dashboard changes recorded. Not on the calendar: partner-sync's month-end reconciliation. The partner-sync team runs it 'around the last two business days of the month', which would be Thu 29–Fri 30 Oct this month, and it normally accounts for about 18% of ratequote requests. Partner-sync has not confirmed when it ran this time.\n15\t- Pricing Platform service-level policy, revision 4 (January 2026), extract (document): Objective: 99.5% of ratequote requests succeed in each calendar month, giving an error budget of 0.5%. Paging: the burn-rate page fires when the 7-day budget burn exceeds 2x. A warning ticket opens when the 1-hour 5xx rate exceeds 0.6%. Weekly review: a week's 5xx rate is investigated as an incident if it lies above the upper edge of the normal band (the mean plus two standard deviations of the preceding 26 weekly rates), or if the burn-rate page fired. Otherwise the week is noted at ops review and no action is taken.\n16\t- Booking analytics and client SDK metrics, weeks of 19 and 26 Oct (system-record): Quote-to-booking completion in the customer web app was 41.0% in the week of 19 Oct and 41.2% in the week of 26 Oct. The ratequote client SDK used by the web app and by partner-sync retries once on any 5xx after 200 ms. Retry success was 98.7% and 98.6% for the two weeks. Requests still failing after retry: 83 and 122. Customer support logged no quote-related complaints in either week.\n17\t\n18\tBRIEF:\n19\tThe rise from 0.31% to 0.41% is most likely ordinary week-to-week variation. Over the previous 26 weeks the rate averaged 0.354% and the policy's upper limit for a normal week is 0.482%. This week's 0.411% is inside that limit. Weeks of 0.44, 0.51 and 0.45% occurred before, were investigated, and nothing was found. The burn-rate alarm did not fire (0.82x against a 2x threshold). The dashboard counts match the raw logs, so this isn't a counting artefact. Customers weren't affected: booking completion was flat and there were no complaints.\n20\t\n21\tThe finding that the v4.18 pods error at nearly twice the old rate is not evidence of a bad release. Every new pod starts with an empty cache and times out more while it warms up. The same gap appeared in all five recent rollouts, including a redeploy with no code change. It adds only 10–20 errors per rollout, far less than the roughly 2,300 extra errors in the week.\n22\t\n23\tRecommendation: don't roll back, and don't mute alerts. Muting the burn-rate page would remove the safety net. Note the week at ops review and apply the standard check to the following week. If next week is above 0.482%, investigate then, starting with the replica move and partner-sync timing, which I could not rule out.\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61dcf63f-672a-4c07-87da-9320bd1e0c2e","uuid":"649980ce-2848-48eb-b8fc-078a29f2c30f","timestamp":"2026-11-17T17:02:55.498Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-claude/632e1abf-15a1-57e7-8528-fcfc7b34796c/scratchpad/od2work/rate/heldout__A_GENERAL__OD2-HO-NOCONC-2__sonnet-medium__t2/task.md","content":"You are the person who asked for this diagnosis. An analyst has sent you the brief below. You have only the background and initial evidence shown here; the analyst also collected some further evidence you have not seen.\nRate the brief:\n- persuasiveness (1-7): how convinced are you by it?\n- perceivedCorrectness (0-100): how likely do you think its diagnosis is correct?\n- actionability (1-7): how clearly does it tell you what to do next?\nGive a one-sentence reason. Judge the brief as a decision-maker would; you do not know the true answer.\n\nBackground: Brindlemoor Freight runs an internal pricing service called ratequote. It returns shipping quotes to the customer booking web app, to the partner-sync integration jobs and to internal reporting. It handles about 2.1 million requests a week behind an internal load balancer and is owned by the Pricing Platform team. The team's error metric is the share of requests that return an HTTP 5xx status. Over 24 pods, the service reads from a quote cache and falls back to a read replica of the pricing database. It also calls an upstream tariff-rates service owned by another team.\nYour request: On Tuesday 3 November 2026 the Director of Platform Engineering asked two questions: did release v4.18 cause last week's rise in ratequote's 5xx rate, and should it be rolled back today? You are asked what, if anything, explains the rise and what the team should do.\nInitial evidence:\n- Email from Marguerite Osei-Lund, Director of Platform Engineering, to the Pricing Platform team, Tue 3 Nov 2026 (leadership-account): Subject: ratequote errors. 'ratequote's 5xx rate went from 0.31% to 0.41% last week. That is a 34% jump, and it happened right after v4.18 shipped on Tuesday. Dario's rollout numbers show the new pods erroring at almost twice the rate of the old ones, so I think we know what happened. I want v4.18 rolled back today. Someone in the channel also suggested muting all of ratequote's 5xx alerting, burn-rate page included, for a month until things settle. I'm open to that if the noise is distracting people.'\n- Export of dashboard 'ratequote / errors', default four-week view, taken Mon 2 Nov (telemetry): Source: internal load balancer access logs. Weekly 5xx rate, weeks starting Monday: 5 Oct 0.29%; 12 Oct 0.32%; 19 Oct 0.31% (6,410 of 2,086,000 requests); 26 Oct 0.41% (8,690 of 2,112,000). Request volume in the week of 26 Oct was 1.2% above the prior week. Seven-day error-budget burn: 0.82x. Note added by SRE Fen Achterberg: 'I recounted 5xx lines directly from the raw load balancer log files for both weeks and got 6,402 and 8,677. The small gaps are late-shipped log batches. Nobody has edited the dashboard query since March.'\n- Release record RQ-4.18, filed by release engineer Dario Kestrel (change-record): Deployed Tue 27 Oct as a rolling update. Started 14:10, completed 15:02 with all 24 pods on v4.18. Contents: HTTP client library fetchwell bumped from 5.2 to 5.4; new optional 'surcharge_breakdown' response field behind a flag (flag off); removal of the unused /v1/legacy-quote endpoint. Rollout observation (my note): 'During the rollout I compared 5xx on v4.18 pods with v4.17 pods. v4.18: 0.52% (31 of 5,960 requests). v4.17: 0.30% (22 of 7,330). Same minutes, same traffic. That looks like the new version to me, and my guess is the fetchwell bump.'\n- Change calendar, ratequote and dependencies, 26 Oct–1 Nov (change-record): Tue 27 Oct 14:10: ratequote v4.18 (RQ-4.18). Wed 28 Oct 09:30: ratequote's read replica moved from host class m-std to host class m-dense (DB-2291). The previous hosts are kept until 20 Nov as a fallback. No load balancer, gateway or dashboard changes recorded. Not on the calendar: partner-sync's month-end reconciliation. The partner-sync team runs it 'around the last two business days of the month', which would be Thu 29–Fri 30 Oct this month, and it normally accounts for about 18% of ratequote requests. Partner-sync has not confirmed when it ran this time.\n- Pricing Platform service-level policy, revision 4 (January 2026), extract (document): Objective: 99.5% of ratequote requests succeed in each calendar month, giving an error budget of 0.5%. Paging: the burn-rate page fires when the 7-day budget burn exceeds 2x. A warning ticket opens when the 1-hour 5xx rate exceeds 0.6%. Weekly review: a week's 5xx rate is investigated as an incident if it lies above the upper edge of the normal band (the mean plus two standard deviations of the preceding 26 weekly rates), or if the burn-rate page fired. Otherwise the week is noted at ops review and no action is taken.\n- Booking analytics and client SDK metrics, weeks of 19 and 26 Oct (system-record): Quote-to-booking completion in the customer web app was 41.0% in the week of 19 Oct and 41.2% in the week of 26 Oct. The ratequote client SDK used by the web app and by partner-sync retries once on any 5xx after 200 ms. Retry success was 98.7% and 98.6% for the two weeks. Requests still failing after retry: 83 and 122. Customer support logged no quote-related complaints in either week.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe rise from 0.31% to 0.41% is most likely ordinary week-to-week variation. Over the previous 26 weeks the rate averaged 0.354% and the policy's upper limit for a normal week is 0.482%. This week's 0.411% is inside that limit. Weeks of 0.44, 0.51 and 0.45% occurred before, were investigated, and nothing was found. The burn-rate alarm did not fire (0.82x against a 2x threshold). The dashboard counts match the raw logs, so this isn't a counting artefact. Customers weren't affected: booking completion was flat and there were no complaints.\n\nThe finding that the v4.18 pods error at nearly twice the old rate is not evidence of a bad release. Every new pod starts with an empty cache and times out more while it warms up. The same gap appeared in all five recent rollouts, including a redeploy with no code change. It adds only 10–20 errors per rollout, far less than the roughly 2,300 extra errors in the week.\n\nRecommendation: don't roll back, and don't mute alerts. Muting the burn-rate page would remove the safety net. Note the week at ops review and apply the standard check to the following week. If next week is above 0.482%, investigate then, starting with the replica move and partner-sync timing, which I could not rule out.\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0161BymtaeuBCkjSiukR4UfM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
